{"tag":"CKL5","kind":"statement","subkind":"corollary","paper":"2610.12819","version":"1","local":"head-6","file":"","number":"1.4","section":"sec-7","order":6,"title":"Corollary","body_md":"{\\em For $q=5$, we construct a sequence of linear $5$-ary minimal codes with the rate at least $\\frac{11}{864}$ explicitly.}","path":"source","confidence":"high","env":"head","item":"statement/corollary","head":"manual","number_from":"head","scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"smaller-strong.tex","from":174,"to":174},"hash":"sha256:f24231d132b3797305b2439c8fd83a348f44367eb270f7ac3939dbbcf130c70b","minhash":"","census":"2610.12819v1#head-6","extractor":"ax/v0.5.29"}
{"tag":"ZGEV","kind":"statement","subkind":"theorem","paper":"2610.12819","version":"1","local":"head-7","file":"","number":"2.1","section":"sec-8","order":7,"title":"Theorem","body_md":"{\\em For any given square prime power $q^{2m}$, we have a linear $(q-1)$-power intersecting algebraic code ${\\bf C}({\\bf X}, {|bf G}, {\\bf P})$ over ${\\bf F}_{q^{2m}}$ if $\\deg({\\bf G}) \u003c \\frac{n}{2(q-1)}$. Then we have a sequence of $q-1$-power intersecting algebraic geometric $q^{2m}$-ary codes with the rate at least $\\frac{1}{q-1} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{q^m-1})$.}","path":"source","confidence":"high","env":"head","item":"statement/theorem","head":"manual","number_from":"head","scope":"paper","proof_status":"elsewhere","origin":"own","span":{"file":"smaller-strong.tex","from":188,"to":188},"hash":"sha256:fb84d127da6f29c9927df6c14132ad9ac91b7b4c0930ae4400648dc49f481117","minhash":"","census":"2610.12819v1#head-7","extractor":"ax/v0.5.29"}
{"tag":"P96Y","kind":"statement","subkind":"theorem","paper":"2610.12819","version":"1","local":"head-8","file":"","number":"3.1","section":"sec-9","order":8,"title":"Theorem","body_md":"{\\em Let ${\\bf C}_1 \\subset {\\bf F}_{q^m}^N$ be a $q^m$-ary $K$-dimensional linear code such that ${\\bf C}_1^{q-1}\\subset {\\bf F}_{q^m}^N$ is an intersecting code. We assume that there is an explicit $[n,m]_q$ minimal $q$-ary code ${\\bf C}_2 \\in {\\bf F}_q^n$. Then we can construct a minimal linear $[Nn,Km]_q$ code in ${\\bf F}_q^{Nn}$.}","path":"source","confidence":"high","env":"head","item":"statement/theorem","head":"manual","number_from":"head","scope":"paper","proof_status":"here","origin":"own","span":{"file":"smaller-strong.tex","from":199,"to":199},"hash":"sha256:3a43838af1e6c524fcfb6fa481827a2bbd2fba63eddb7587fb5e188b9556eff7","minhash":"","census":"2610.12819v1#head-8","extractor":"ax/v0.5.29"}
{"tag":"SQ29","kind":"proof","paper":"2610.12819","version":"1","local":"head-9","file":"","section":"sec-9","order":9,"title":"Proof","body_md":"Let ${\\bf c}_1=(c_{0,1}, \\ldots, c_{N-1,1})$ and ${\\bf c}_2=(c_{0,2}, \\ldots, c_{N-1,2})$ be two nonzero codewords of the $q^m$-ary linear ${\\bf C}_1$ such that, $supp(\\Phi({\\bf c}_1)) \\subset supp(\\Phi({\\bf c}_2)).$ Then for each coordinate position $i$, $0 \\leq i \\leq N-1$, we have $supp(\\phi(c_{i,1})) \\subset supp(\\phi(c_{i,2}))$. Since $\\phi(c_{i,1})$ and $\\phi(c_{i,2})$ are two nonzero codewords of the $q$-ary minimal ${\\bf C}_2$, then there exists a nonzero element $\\lambda_i \\in {\\bf F}_q^*$, such that $$c_{i,1}=\\lambda_i c_{i,2}.$$\n\nIt is clear that $$\\lambda_i^{q-1} c_{i,2}^{q-1}=c_{i,1}^{q-1}=c_{i,2}^{q-1},$$ since $\\lambda_i \\in {\\bf F}_q^*$ and $\\lambda_i^{q-1}=1$. Then we have two nonzero codewords ${\\bf c}_1'$ and ${\\bf c}_2'$ in ${\\bf C}_1^{q-1}$, such that, $$supp({\\bf c}_1) \\subset supp({\\bf c}_2),$$ and at the coordinate positions of $supp({\\bf c}_1')$, the coordinates of these two nonzero codewords at this position are the same. Then ${\\bf c}_2'-{\\bf c}_1'$ is a nonzero codeword of ${\\bf C}_1^{q-1}$ such that $supp({\\bf c}_2'-{\\bf c}_1') \\bigcap supp({\\bf c}_1')$ is empty. This is a contradiction to the assumption that ${\\bf C}_1^{q-1}$ is intersecting. Then the concatenated code is minimal.\\\\","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"head","item":"proof","head":"manual","scope":"paper","proves":"head-8","attach":3,"span":{"file":"smaller-strong.tex","from":201,"to":204},"hash":"sha256:0d8ce71dd7336dac1dde3b5e9e540ce0a267593adaf824a4fbede1e626a2ae93","minhash":"","census":"2610.12819v1#proof-1","extractor":"ax/v0.5.29"}
{"tag":"G379","kind":"statement","subkind":"corollary","paper":"2610.12819","version":"1","local":"head-10","file":"","number":"3.1","section":"sec-9","order":10,"title":"Corollary","body_md":"{\\em Let ${\\bf C}_1 \\subset {\\bf F}_{q^m}^N$ be a $q^m$-ary $K$-dimensional linear code such that ${\\bf C}_1^{q-1}\\subset {\\bf F}_{q^m}^N$ is an intersecting code. We assume that there is an explicit $[n,m]_q$ minimal $q$-ary code ${\\bf C}_2 \\in {\\bf F}_q^n$. Then we construct a strong blocking set in $P({\\bf F}_q, Km-1)$ with the size $Nn$.}","path":"source","confidence":"high","env":"head","item":"statement/corollary","head":"manual","number_from":"head","scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"smaller-strong.tex","from":205,"to":205},"hash":"sha256:9d0dd73eddb5210f51fbb0d115c88c1835983afbb90c4ffa83c00d524e463fa8","minhash":"","census":"2610.12819v1#head-9","extractor":"ax/v0.5.29"}
{"tag":"9OO1","kind":"statement","subkind":"corollary","paper":"2610.12819","version":"1","local":"head-11","file":"","number":"3.2","section":"sec-9","order":11,"title":"Corollary","body_md":"{\\em Let ${\\bf C}_1$ be a $q^m$-ary rate $R_1$ linear code such that ${\\bf C}_1^{q-1}$ is an intersecting code. We assume that there is an explicit rate $R_2$ minimal $q$-ary code ${\\bf C}_2$. Then we construct a $q$-ary minimal code with the rate $R_1 R_2$.}","path":"source","confidence":"high","env":"head","item":"statement/corollary","head":"manual","number_from":"head","scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"smaller-strong.tex","from":207,"to":207},"hash":"sha256:1cfe3b46da135a956834bfc6f50863c69844a8ca6f8c739c08d8807ada7c1c0f","minhash":"","census":"2610.12819v1#head-10","extractor":"ax/v0.5.29"}
{"tag":"G5TD","kind":"statement","subkind":"corollary","paper":"2610.12819","version":"1","local":"head-12","file":"","number":"3.3","section":"sec-9","order":12,"title":"Corollary","body_md":"{\\em Suppose that $q$ is an arbitrary prime power and $m \\geq 2$ is a positive integer. We assume that there exists an explicit $[n,2m]_q$ minimal code. Then we construct a sequence of strong blocking sets $S_i \\subset P({\\bf F}_q, k_i-1)$, where $k_i$ goes to the infinity,  such that $$\\lim \\frac{|S_i|}{k_i}\\leq \\frac{n(q^m-1)(q-1)}{m(q^m-3)}.$$ Equivalently, we construct sequences of minimal $q$-ary codes with rate at least $\\frac{m(q^m-3)}{n(q-1)(q^m-1)}$.}","path":"source","confidence":"high","env":"head","item":"statement/corollary","head":"manual","number_from":"head","scope":"paper","proof_status":"here","origin":"own","span":{"file":"smaller-strong.tex","from":210,"to":210},"hash":"sha256:51baef4c7285644ad875a3c87d79dbfd6e35fc163a8bb02627aad52263c6361d","minhash":"","census":"2610.12819v1#head-11","extractor":"ax/v0.5.29"}
